  "dom_id": "questionExplanation-338766",
  "name": null,
  "problem": "",
  "worked_solution": "From the fundamental theorem of calculus, we find that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-4}^{4}{f}^{′}(x)\\,\\text{d}x & =f(4)-f(-4) \\\\ {\\int }_{-4}^{4}{f}^{′}(x)\\,\\text{d}x & =12-f(-4) \\\\ f(-4) & =12-{\\int }_{-4}^{4}{f}^{′}(x)\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nRemember that the integral represents the signed area between the curve and the $x$-axis.\n\nThe graph consists of $2$ parts on $[-4,4]:$ the line segments for $x\\in [-4,-2]$ and two line segments for $x\\in [-2,4].$ Therefore, we can write the integral as follows:\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{-4}^{4}{f}^{′}(x)\\,\\text{d}x={{{\\int }_{-4}^{-2}{f}^{′}(x)\\,\\text{d}x}_{⏟}}_{-{A}_{1}}+{{{\\int }_{-2}^{4}{f}^{′}(x)\\,\\text{d}x}_{⏟}}_{{A}_{2}}\n$$\n\nThe first integral ${A}_{1}$ represents the signed area between the line segment and the $x$-axis, where $x\\in [-4,-2].$ The corresponding region is a right triangle, so we find the area using the formula\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}_{1} & =\\frac{1}{2}bh \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}(2)(2) \\\\  & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHowever, since the region lies below the $x$-axis, we take the area as negative:\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{-4}^{-2}{f}^{′}(x)\\,\\text{d}x=-{A}_{1}=-2\n$$\n\nThe second integral ${A}_{2}$ represents the signed area between the curve and the $x$-axis, where $x\\in [-2,4].$ The corresponding region is a right triangle, so we find the area using the formula\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}_{2} & =\\frac{1}{2}bh \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}(6)(2) \\\\  & =6.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the value of the integral is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-4}^{4}{f}^{′}(x)\\,\\text{d}x & =-{A}_{1}+{A}_{2} \\\\  & =-2+6 \\\\  & =4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, using the value of the integral, we can find $f(-4):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(-4) & =12-{\\int }_{-4}^{4}{f}^{′}(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =12-4 \\\\  & =8\\end{aligned}\n$$",
